  "text": "None of the three solutions gives the correct value, which I believe is 6. I could not verify this against the literature here, so this rests on my recollection of published results on this problem.\n\n- **Solution 1 (7):** The lower-bound sketch only shows that iterated rotations produce \"at least 7\" longest paths, and the matching construction is asserted without being given. A 3-regular gadget that yields exactly 7 is never exhibited, and 7 is not a value I recall for this problem.\n- **Solution 2 (4):** The claim that every minimum-degree-3 graph has at least 4 longest paths sharing a vertex is asserted without a source, and the cited \"Walther graph\" is a known construction for a different question (it is not a standard example of a graph with exactly 4 longest paths on 18 vertices). The argument does not establish the lower bound.\n- **Solution 3 (36):** The lower-bound argument is hand-waved (\"rules out closures isomorphic to C₃, C₄, C₅, K₂,₃\" is stated, not shown). The K₄-trap construction does produce 36 longest paths, but that only shows 36 is an upper bound. A cheaper architecture, which I believe exists for this problem, gives fewer, so 36 is not the minimum.\n\nThe value I recall for this problem is a(3,n) = 6 for n ≥ 18, and no proposed solution reaches it.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
